      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">Frequently asked questions about hourly wage UK</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <Accordion title="How do I calculate my hourly rate from my salary?">
          <p>
            Divide your annual gross salary by 52 (weeks in a year), then divide by your hours per week. For example: £30,000 ÷ 52 = £576.92/week ÷ 37.5 hours = £15.38/hour. Our salary to hourly rate calculator above does this automatically.
          </p>
        </Accordion>
        <Accordion title="What is the UK average hourly wage 2026?">
          <p>
            The UK median hourly wage in 2026 is approximately £15.50–£17.00 per hour, depending on the source and region. The National Living Wage for workers aged 21+ is £12.71/hour from April 2026. Use our hourly wage calculator to see what any rate converts to as an annual salary.
          </p>
        </Accordion>
        <Accordion title="Is £15 an hour a good salary in the UK?">
          <p>
            At £15/hour for 37.5 hours per week, your annual salary is £29,250. After tax, NI and a 5% pension, you'd take home roughly £23,800 per year (£1,983/month). This is above the UK median and considered a solid wage outside London.
          </p>
        </Accordion>
        <Accordion title="How many hours is full-time in the UK?">
          <p>
            There is no legal definition of full-time in the UK, but it is typically 35–40 hours per week. The standard working week is 37.5 hours (e.g. 9am–5:30pm with a lunch break). Our hourly wage calculator uses 37.5 hours as the default.
          </p>
        </Accordion>
        <Accordion title="Does being paid hourly change how much tax I pay?">
          <p>
            No. HMRC taxes your total annual income, so whether you are salaried or paid by the hour, the same 2026/27 income tax, National Insurance and student loan rules apply to your gross annual salary.
          </p>
        </Accordion>
        <Accordion title="Is overtime taxed more than my normal hours?">
          <p>
            Overtime is added to your annual gross and taxed at your marginal rate — the rate your highest earnings fall into. You never pay 100% tax on extra hours, but extra earnings can push you into a higher tax band.
          </p>
        </Accordion>
